gnugo on OS-9 -- what was changed==================================GNU GO version 1.1, 1 March 1989 -- the Free Software Foundation's Goprogram, written by Man L. Li, modified by Wayne Iba, documented by BobWebber.  It plays Go (Wei-Chi) on a 19x19 board, takes a handicap, and willcount the score at the end.NOTHING IN THE C WAS CHANGED, and that is worth saying plainly because thetree here is not the Usenet posting.The program was posted to comp.sources.games as v06i019 in 1989.  FrankKaefer ported it to OS-9 the same year -- `modified for OS-9 4/15/89fkk@stasys.UUCP' in the makefile -- and TOP, the Munich OS-9 Project,shipped his tree with a working OSK binary beside it.  What is here is histree, and the whole of his port is in the makefile:    .o  ->  .r                       object suffix    CFLAGS = -O   ->  -qt=/dd        temporary directory    $(CC) $(OBJ) -o $@  ->  -fd=$@   module outputNot one line of C differs from the posting.  Compared file by file with thecomp.sources.games text, all 26 sources are identical once the line endingsare set aside -- his copies are CR-terminated, the posting's are LF.ORIG/ holds his tree as TOP shipped it, `Makefile.orig' included, so thetwo makefiles can be read side by side.TWO THINGS THE RECIPE HAS TO KNOW.1.  `patterns.c' IS NOT COMPILED.  matchpat.c includes it as a header --    `matchpat.r : patterns.c' is the only mention in the makefile -- so the    recipe lists 26 sources and patterns.c is not among them.  Listing it    would compile the pattern tables twice.2.  <sys/time.h> IS NEVER REACHED.  seed.c includes it inside `#ifdef SUN',    and nothing defines SUN here.It compiled clean first try.  The one diagnostic is a `redefined macro' inpatterns.c, which is harmless.FOR COMPARISON.  TOP's 1989 build is 42,766 bytes; this one is 43,994 --the same program through a different compiler.  Their binary is not shippedhere; this is built from the source.WHAT IT NEEDS TO RUN.  Nothing.  It asks for a handicap and a side, thentakes moves as a letter and a number (`D4'), and needs no terminal setup.